issue.body:
## Summary

Reading from `@io.MemoryReader` (or the in-memory `@io.pipe()`) moves at most 1016 bytes per `read()`, and every transfer costs one scheduler round. On the js target a round that has newly woken tasks is deferred with `setTimeout(0)`, which Node clamps to about 1 ms. So reading 2.6 MB takes about 3 seconds on js, almost all of it waiting: the process uses 0.18 s of CPU in 5 s of wall time. Native is also slower than it needs to be (35 ms for 2.6 MB, about 75 MB/s).

## Reproduction

```moonbit
// moon.pkg: import "moonbitlang/core/async", "moonbitlang/core/async/io",
// "moonbitlang/core/bench"; pkgtype(kind: "executable");
// options("allow-experimental-packages": true)

///|
async fn run(chunks : Int, size : Int) -> Unit {
  let data = Bytes::make(size, b'x')
  let start = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
  let reader = @io.MemoryReader(w => for _ in 0..<chunks {
    w.write(data)
  })
  defer reader.close()
  let mut total = 0
  let mut reads = 0
  let mut largest = 0
  while reader.read() is Some(b) {
    total += b.length()
    reads += 1
    largest = largest.max(b.length())
  }
  let ms = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(start) / 1000.0
  println("chunks=\{chunks} size=\{size}: \{ms} ms, \{total} bytes, reads=\{reads} largest=\{largest}")
}

///|
async fn main {
  for size in [1024, 16384, 65536] {
    for chunks in [10, 40] {
      run(chunks, size)
    }
  }
}
```

`moon run --target js --release cmd/main` and `--target native`, Apple Silicon, moon 0.1.20261104, moonc v0.10.20 (2026-11-05), Node v25.8.2:

| writes | bytes | reads | largest read | js | native |
| ------ | ----- | ----- | ------------ | -- | ------ |
| 10 x 1 KiB  | 10 KiB  | 20   | 1016 | 30 ms   | 0.3 ms |
| 40 x 1 KiB  | 40 KiB  | 80   | 1016 | 96 ms   | 1.1 ms |
| 10 x 16 KiB | 160 KiB | 170  | 1016 | 200 ms  | 2.3 ms |
| 40 x 16 KiB | 640 KiB | 680  | 1016 | 781 ms  | 9.1 ms |
| 10 x 64 KiB | 640 KiB | 650  | 1016 | 739 ms  | 8.9 ms |
| 40 x 64 KiB | 2.5 MiB | 2600 | 1016 | 2968 ms | 35 ms  |

Each 1 KiB write takes two reads (1016 + 8 bytes). On js every `read()` costs 1.1 to 1.5 ms, however the writer chunks the data. `/usr/bin/time` for the whole js run: real 5.0 s, user 0.18 s. Same result on core main (70af5a09c) with a moonc built from compiler master.

## Cause

1. **Every read is at most one 1 KiB segment.** `BinaryReader::read` calls `peek`, which calls `ensure_buffered(1)`. `BinaryReaderController::enlarge_to` (async/io/binary_reader_controller.mbt, `SEGMENT_SIZE = 1024`) grows the empty buffer to one segment, so `raw_read_impl` is called with `max_len` of about 1016. `PipeRead::raw_read_impl` (async/io/pipe.mbt) copies `min(max_len, writer.len)`, wakes the writer and does `@coroutine.pause_nocancel()`. A 64 KiB `write` is therefore delivered in 64 rounds.
2. **On js, each such round goes through `setTimeout(0)`.** `reschedule` in async/internal/event_loop/event_loop.js.mbt runs one round of ready coroutines and, if more became ready, does `set_timeout(0, reschedule)`. Node clamps `setTimeout(0)` to 1 ms (browsers clamp to 4 ms after 5 nested timers), so a ping-pong between a reader and a writer runs at about 1000 rounds per second.

## Suggestions

- Deliver a pipe write in one read when possible: for example, size the buffer for `read()`/`peek()` to the pending writer's remaining length (or a larger default, such as 64 KiB, as `read_all`'s `chunk_size` suggests), or give `PipeRead`/`MemoryReader` a `read()` that returns the writer's remaining slice directly.
- On js, run immediately ready tasks without a timer clamp: `setImmediate` on Node, or a `MessageChannel` post in browsers, and keep starvation protection with a time or round budget before falling back to `setTimeout`.

Either change alone removes most of the gap; the first also helps native.

Found while benchmarking a streaming JSON parser (https://github.com/bobzhang/ajson) that reads bytes through `MemoryReader`: on js, the reader took about 3 s of a 3.2 s parse of 2.4 MB, against 141 ms when the same parser is fed string chunks directly.

## Summary

On the native backend, each call to `@bench.monotonic_clock_start()` allocates 32 bytes that are never freed.
Calling `monotonic_clock_end` on the timestamp does not free it either.
A program that times every request with it grows without bound.

## Environment

- moon 0.1.20261023 (3778465 2026-10-23)
- moonc v0.10.17+d971670cd (2026-10-23)
- moonrun 0.1.20261023 (3778465 2026-10-23)
- Target: native, both `--release` and `--debug`
- OS: Linux aarch64 (Asahi Linux)

It also happens with moonc v0.10.14+7d59c7ec9 and moon 0.1.20260920 (native, `--release`).

## Reproduction

`moon.mod`:

```
name = "repro/clockleak"

preferred_target = "native"
```

`main/moon.pkg`:

```
import {
  "moonbitlang/core/bench",
  "moonbitlang/core/env",
}

options(
  "is-main": true,
  "native-stub": [ "rss.c" ],
)
```

`main/rss.c` reads VmRSS from `/proc/self/status`:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>

// VmRSS of /proc/self/status, in KiB.
int repro_rss_kib(void) {
  FILE *f = fopen("/proc/self/status", "r");
  char line[256];
  int kib = -1;
  if (!f) return -1;
  while (fgets(line, sizeof line, f)) {
    if (strncmp(line, "VmRSS:", 6) == 0) { sscanf(line + 6, "%d", &kib); break; }
  }
  fclose(f);
  return kib;
}
```

`main/main.mbt`:

```mbt
///|
extern "C" fn rss_kib() -> Int32 = "repro_rss_kib"

///|
fn main {
  let args = @env.args()
  let mode = if args.length() > 1 { args[1] } else { "leak" }
  let n = 1_000_000
  let before = rss_kib()
  let mut sum = 0.0
  match mode {
    // Call start and drop the result.
    "leak" =>
      for _ in 0..<n {
        ignore(@bench.monotonic_clock_start())
      }
    // The usual pattern: start, then end on the same timestamp.
    "start_end" =>
      for _ in 0..<n {
        let t = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
        sum += @bench.monotonic_clock_end(t)
      }
    // Workaround: one origin, read with end.
    "workaround" => {
      let origin = @bench.monotonic_clock_start()
      for _ in 0..<n {
        sum += @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin)
      }
    }
    _ => abort("unknown mode")
  }
  let after = rss_kib()
  let per_call = (after - before).to_double() * 1024.0 / n.to_double()
  println(
    "\{mode}: VmRSS \{before} KiB -> \{after} KiB (+\{after - before} KiB), \{per_call} B/call, sum>=0: \{sum >= 0.0}",
  )
}
```

Run each mode with `--release` and with `--debug`:

```
moon run main --target native --release -- leak
moon run main --target native --release -- start_end
moon run main --target native --release -- workaround
```

## Expected

VmRSS stays flat. Each `Timestamp` is dropped after its iteration.

## Actual

Growth of VmRSS over 1,000,000 iterations:

| Mode | `--release` | `--debug` |
|---|---|---|
| `leak` (start only) | +31,504 KiB, 32.3 B/call | +31,520 KiB, 32.3 B/call |
| `start_end` (start, then end) | +31,520 KiB, 32.3 B/call | +31,536 KiB, 32.3 B/call |
| `workaround` (one origin) | +256 KiB, 0.26 B/call | +272 KiB, 0.28 B/call |

## Workaround

Call `monotonic_clock_start()` once, at startup, and keep the result as an origin.
Read times with `monotonic_clock_end(origin)` and subtract.

```mbt
let origin = @bench.monotonic_clock_start() // once
let t0 = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin)
// ... work ...
let elapsed_us = @bench.monotonic_clock_end(origin) - t0
```

## Cause

From reading the source on `main` and the generated C:

- core declares the type as `#external type Timestamp` ([bench/monotonic_clock_native.mbt#L16-L17](https://github.com/moonbitlang/core/blob/df5289b8fc98d31258b507abca24bff45cae555d/bench/monotonic_clock_native.mbt#L16-L17)).
  An `#external` value is a raw pointer, so MoonBit never increfs or decrefs it.
- The runtime allocates the timestamp with `moonbit_make_external_object` ([env.c#L812-L819](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moonbit-native-runtime/blob/88e2a69ffff2f3faae07d2a94a9f7e54808cc2d1/env.c#L812-L819)).
  That is a reference-counted object: an 8-byte header, a 16-byte `timespec`, and an 8-byte finalizer pointer, 32 bytes in all.
  Only `moonbit_decref` can free it.
- The generated C for `start_end` has no `moonbit_decref` on the timestamp:

```c
void* _M0L1tS918 = _M0FPC15bench23monotonic__clock__start();
double _M0L6_2atmpS1104 = _M0Lm3sumS914;
double _M0L6_2atmpS1105 =
  _M0FPC15bench21monotonic__clock__end(_M0L1tS918);
```

So nothing ever frees it.
The Windows branch ([env.c#L777-L782](https://github.com/moonbitlang/moonbit-native-runtime/blob/88e2a69ffff2f3faae07d2a94a9f7e54808cc2d1/env.c#L777-L782)) allocates the same way, so I expect it to leak too.
I tested only native on Linux. I did not test Windows, wasm, wasm-gc, or JS.

Possible fixes (untested):

- Drop `#external`, so that MoonBit reference-counts `Timestamp`. This matches what the runtime allocates. `monotonic_clock_end` would need to borrow `ts`.
- Or make the timestamp a plain integer (for example, nanoseconds as `UInt64`), so that nothing is allocated.

