### Proposal Details

I'm proposing to extend the syntax of switch statement expressions to allow cases to contain an optional initialization statement. They should be evaluated immediately before their corresponding case expressions.

Proposed grammar mirrors the existing `if <initializer>; <expression> { ... }` syntax:

```
ExprCaseClause = "case" [ SimpleStmt ";" ] ExpressionList ":" StatementList .
```

The scope semantics would be equivalent to if-statements: scopes of vars introduced in each case initializer extend to subsequent case expressions and their corresponding statements. This follows the same visibility semantics as `if` initializer across an `if / else if / else` chain.

### Example

```go
func ActivateUser(id int) error {
    switch {
    case user, err := db.GetUser(id); err != nil:
        return fmt.Errorf("get user: %w", err)

    case permissions, err := auth.GetPermissions(user); err != nil:
        return fmt.Errorf("get permissions: %w", err)

    case !permissions.CanActivate:
        return ErrForbidden

    case activation, err := db.ActivateUser(user.ID); err != nil:
        return fmt.Errorf("activate user: %w", err)

    case err := email.Send(user.Email, activation.Token); err != nil:
        slog.Error("send user email failed", "email", user.Email, "error", err)
    }

    return nil
}
```

Equivalent version with current Go syntax:

```go
func ActivateUser(id int64) error {
    user, err := db.GetUser(id)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("get user: %w", err)
    }

    permissions, err := auth.GetPermissions(user)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("get permissions: %w", err)
    }

    if !permissions.CanActivate {
        return ErrForbidden
    }

    activation, err := db.ActivateUser(user.ID)
    if err != nil {
        return fmt.Errorf("activate user: %w", err)
    }

    if err := email.Send(user.Email, activation.Token); err != nil {
        slog.Error("send user email failed", "email", user.Email, "error", err)
    }

    return nil
}
```

### Motivation & benefits

- This adopts the existing well-known `if stmt; expr { ... }` syntax.
- `switch` already provides a structure for a sequence of mutually exclusive conditions, and per-case initializers would make that structure more expressive.
- This is visually better aligned than the chain of if-else statements.
- Cases that evaluate to true naturally interrupt the chain without the need for an explicit `return` or `goto` by building on top of well-known and widely used `switch` semantics.
- Less nesting, flatter control flow.
- The code reads top to bottom naturally as a sequence of steps: fetch user, validate, activate, send email. The majority of the code that I work with is a sequence of operations with early returns and linearly increasing number of variables.
- No need to declare temporary variables upfront with `var` statements: variables are introduced exactly where they're needed.
- It's not just a way to handle errors, but a generic way to do conditional initialization in a compact way:
    ```go
    switch {
    case user, ok := users[id]; !ok:
        return ErrNotFound

    case !user.IsActive:
        return ErrInactive

    case age := calculateAge(user); age < 18:
        return ErrUnderage
    }
    ```
- The proposal does NOT aim to "solve" error handling or introduce implicit error propagation or hidden control flow, but rather extend the `switch` statement to gain parity with `if` syntax.

`elf_add` follows two section indexes that a corrupt file can set out of range:

- `e_shstrndx` (or, for `SHN_XINDEX`, the `sh_link` of section 0) names the section name table and is checked only against zero. An index that is not below the number of sections makes `elf_add` read a section header from beyond the end of the table.
- The `sh_link` of a symbol table is checked against the number of sections but not against zero. A link of zero becomes the index `0 - 1`, which in unsigned arithmetic lies far beyond the table on a 64-bit host.

In both cases the program crashes at its first lookup. This patch reports both through the error callback and gives up on the file, as `elf_add` already does when a section name is out of range.

I use libbacktrace as the symbolizer of a function tracer, [call-stack-logger](https://github.com/TomaszAugustyn/call-stack-logger), which registers object files itself through `elf_add`. Its tests feed it damaged copies of a plugin, and a copy with `e_shstrndx` set to 0xfeff crashed the traced program. I found the missing zero check while preparing this patch.

To reproduce it with plain libbacktrace, run the program below on a damaged copy of itself. For the section name index, set `e_shstrndx` to 0xfeff (offset 0x3e of an ELF64 header):

```sh
gcc -g -I<libbacktrace source> repro.c <libbacktrace build>/.libs/libbacktrace.a -o repro
cp repro bad
printf '\377\376' | dd of=bad bs=1 seek=62 conv=notrunc
./repro bad
```

For the string table link, set the `sh_link` of `.symtab` (offset 40 of its section header) to 0:

```sh
cp repro bad
shoff=$(LC_ALL=C readelf -hW bad | sed -n 's/.*Start of section headers: *\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')
idx=$(LC_ALL=C readelf -SW bad | sed -n 's/.*\[ *\([0-9]*\)\] \.symtab .*/\1/p')
printf '\000\000\000\000' | dd of=bad bs=1 seek=$((shoff + idx * 64 + 40)) conv=notrunc
./repro bad
```

Before the patch, both copies crash the program with SIGSEGV. With the patch, it prints this for the first copy:

```
error callback: ELF section header string table index out of range (0)
backtrace_pcinfo returned 0
```

and this for the second:

```
error callback: ELF symbol table strtab link out of range (0)
backtrace_pcinfo returned 0
```

An intact copy resolves as before (`pcinfo: repro.c:38 main`). `make check` gives the same result with and without the patch: 45 tests pass, and `strippedtest_stripped` fails on this host (Fedora 44, GCC 16.2.1, binutils 2.46.1) either way.

<details>
<summary>repro.c</summary>

```c
/* Reads the ELF file named on the command line as the executable and
   looks up the address of this program's main.  Run it on a damaged
   copy of itself:

     gcc -g -I<libbacktrace source> repro.c \
	 <libbacktrace build>/.libs/libbacktrace.a -o repro
     cp repro bad
     <damage bad>
     ./repro bad  */

#include <stdint.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
#include "backtrace.h"

static void
error_cb (void *data, const char *msg, int errnum)
{
  (void) data;
  printf ("error callback: %s (%d)\n", msg, errnum);
}

static int
print_cb (void *data, uintptr_t pc, const char *file, int line,
	  const char *function)
{
  (void) data;
  (void) pc;
  if (file != NULL && strrchr (file, '/') != NULL)
    file = strrchr (file, '/') + 1;
  printf ("pcinfo: %s:%d %s\n", file ? file : "?", line,
	  function ? function : "?");
  return 0;
}

int
main (int argc, char **argv)
{
  struct backtrace_state *state;
  int ret;

  if (argc < 2)
    return 2;
  state = backtrace_create_state (argv[1], 0, error_cb, NULL);
  ret = backtrace_pcinfo (state, (uintptr_t) &main, print_cb, error_cb,
			  NULL);
  printf ("backtrace_pcinfo returned %d\n", ret);
  return 0;
}
```

</details>

#29678 adds MarshalText to net.HardwareAddr and net.IPMask in Go 1.28.

#62384 added AppendText to every standard library type with a MarshalText method, and uuid.UUID shipped with both in Go 1.27. These two are now the only types in std with MarshalText but no AppendText:

```go
// AppendText implements the [encoding.TextAppender] interface.
// The encoding is the same as returned by [HardwareAddr.MarshalText].
func (a HardwareAddr) AppendText(b []byte) ([]byte, error)

// AppendText implements the [encoding.TextAppender] interface.
// The encoding is the same as returned by [IPMask.MarshalText].
func (m IPMask) AppendText(b []byte) ([]byte, error)
```

MarshalText would call AppendText, so the netmarshal GODEBUG and the base64 fallback for invalid values stay the same.

