### Reproduction

I was playing with our [verus-tla](https://github.com/anvil-verifier/verus-tla) library and I found Verus was not happy with this broadcast proof:
```Rust
use vstd::prelude::*;

spec fn p() -> bool { true }

broadcast proof fn lemma_p()
    ensures #[trigger] p(),
{}

proof fn caller() {
    lemma_p(); // error: cannot call a broadcast_forall function with 0 arguments directly
}
```
Playground link: https://play.verus-lang.org/?version=stable&mode=basic&edition=2021&gist=3d6d00d4c0c0c9bfd94d07e10b174857

### How can it be helpful
We have a special case where a broadcast lemma with no argument might be helpful, the [current proof](https://github.com/anvil-verifier/verus-tla/blob/84487a0ef8c01e79fa6aaa035476d0970775bac1/src/rules.rs#L1282-L1288) is

```Rust
pub broadcast proof fn true_is_stable<T>()
    ensures valid(stable(true_pred::<T>())),
{
    assert forall |ex| #[trigger] true_pred::<T>().satisfied_by(ex) implies always(true_pred::<T>()).satisfied_by(ex) by {
        assert(true);
    }
}
```

I was playing with [Coalescing](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1409.3819) after reading Murat's [post](https://muratbuffalo.blogspot.com/2026/10/tla-verus-llm-agents-prove-temporal.html), and I want to make FOML operators opaque, so having this lemma broadcast will be helpful.

Please let me know if it's intended and the reason behind this design. I can help on the PR if you also like this case to be supported.

